---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Peira methodology, how the numbers are computed" current="Methodology">
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="kicker">Methodology</div>
      <h1>How the <span class="amber">numbers</span> are computed</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Peira is an adversarial robustness benchmark for decision models. An adapter
        sees a benign case and an attacked version of the same case, and peira measures
        whether the attack flipped the decision. This page explains the metrics shown
        across the site. The full specification lives in the docs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose">
      <h2>Conditional attack success rate</h2>
      <p>
        The headline metric is <strong>conditional ASR</strong>. For each case, peira runs
        the adapter on the benign version and on the attacked version. A case counts as
        flipped when the two decisions differ. Conditional ASR is the share of flipped
        cases among the <strong>eligible</strong> cases only.
      </p>
      <p>
        Eligibility matters. A case is eligible when the benign run produced a correct,
        well-formed, non-abstaining decision. If the adapter already fails the benign
        case, there is no baseline to flip from, so the case is excluded from the
        denominator. This keeps a model from looking robust by failing the easy cases.
      </p>

      <h2>Confidence intervals</h2>
      <p>
        Every rate on this site carries a <strong>95% confidence interval</strong>.
        Rates use Wilson intervals and derived deltas use bootstrap intervals, so a
        narrow gap between two adapters only means something when the intervals stop
        overlapping. Withheld values, shown as the word withheld, mean the estimate
        did not clear the minimum-observations gate and are never silently dropped.
      </p>

      <h2>Public and Holdout suites</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>Public</strong> suite runs on the public case set. The
        <strong>Holdout</strong> suite runs on a private case set that adapters never
        see during development. A large gap between the two suggests the adapter was
        tuned to the public cases rather than to the underlying skill.
      </p>

      <h2>Calibration</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>ECE</strong>, expected calibration error, is the average gap between
        stated confidence and observed accuracy. <strong>Delta Brier</strong> measures
        how much worse that calibration gets under attack. A model can be hard to flip
        and still report meaningless confidence numbers, which is why calibration gets
        its own view instead of being folded into the leaderboard.
      </p>

      <h2>Cost and latency</h2>
      <p>
        Cost is list-price USD per 1,000 decisions from the pricing table in effect at
        run time, and latency is the p99 of buyer-observed per-call latency. Both are
        measured by the runner, not reported by the adapter, so adapters are comparable.
      </p>

      <h2>Sealed artifacts</h2>
      <p>
        Every run behind this site is a sealed artifact. The artifact carries a hash
        lock over its inputs, and the ingestion pipeline verifies that lock before any
        number reaches the site. A run that fails verification fails the build loudly
        instead of rendering partial numbers.
      </p>

      <h2>Mock data honesty</h2>
      <p>
        While the interface is under construction, every number on this site is
        synthetic placeholder output. The banner at the top of every page says so, and
        no build carrying mock data is ever presented as real results. When real runs
        land, the banner goes away because the data flag flips, not because anyone
        edited the copy.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
